  "summary": "Three preprints test whether normalization, unsupervised pruning, and local learning rules determine which computations recurrent networks can sustain.",
  "synthesis": "Three preprints from August 2026 examine how locally applied constraints shape the computations recurrent networks sustain. RDNN shows that divisive normalization produces stable continuous working-memory slow manifolds with low effective rank. Noise-prune, an unsupervised local pruning rule applied to task-trained recurrent networks, preserves performance under sampling and connection rescaling. The layered surprise cascade variant modifies the Forward-Forward objective in a recurrent setting, reporting predictive, cortex-like representations from local learning rules. The items differ in scale and method, so the shared thread is conceptual: normalization and locally applied rules are treated as variables determining which computations a network can readily perform, rather than connectivity alone.",
